<h1>The Euro and Europe's Centralisation Trap</h1>
<blockquote>
<p>Europe's single currency was meant to bring prosperity. Instead, it left many member states trapped in a quagmire of economic stagnation and internal price adjustments.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><img src="/static/shenzhen.jpg" alt="Shenzhen, China - a view from the 71st floor of the Raffles Hotel, 2026" />
View from the 71st floor of the Raffles Hotel, Shenzhen, China, 2026</p>
<h3>A Contemplative Stroll on Hong Kong Island</h3>
<p>Recently, while walking by the window of a real-estate agent in Hong Kong's Wan Chai district, I noticed the listings of HKD24,000,000 (~US$3 million) apartments, unremarkable in every way except price, the likes of which are available in Budapest at a tenth of the cost. It reminded me of two things: firstly that the <em>geoarbitrage</em> remains the most obvious trade right now, and secondly, of the stories of the 1997 Asian Financial Crisis and its ensuing chaos.</p>
<p>In July 1997 the Central Bank of Thailand devalued the baht, triggering massive capital flight, layoffs, bankruptcies and financial pain across the region — what economists describe as a "sudden stop", in which capital flows reverse abruptly and undermine the viability of fixed exchange-rate regimes. Hong Kong's infamously expensive property market, embroiled in the regional panic, collapsed by almost 70%, and reached its nadir in 2003. Underlying structural fragilities had been building for some time. Many regional economies, such as Thailand, Korea, Malaysia and others, had seen short-term USD-denominated debt grow rapidly in their private sectors, encouraged by US dollar pegs (or a basket of currencies dominated by the dollar) which made such borrowing attractive, and concealed the risk of maturity mismatches coupled with fixed but unsustainable exchange rates. Companies had revenue denominated in local currency but debt obligations in dollars they could not earn, and by extension, could not repay.</p>
<p>An economic slowdown was already underway a year earlier in 1996, which began to expose the cracks in the system. Speculators correctly identified the growing current account deficits, mismatched debt obligations, and fundamentally unsustainable dynamics of the Thai baht's peg. They attacked, betting on an inevitable fall. Thailand, contrary to the advice of the likes of Singapore, exhausted billions of dollars in foreign currency reserves defending the baht, but to no avail [1]. The land of smiles saw its currency fall by around half, and falls were seen elsewhere in Indonesia, Korea and Malaysia. The rebalancing was swift and brutal. However - unlike Hong Kong - the recalibration came primarily through the exchange rate, and consumer prices spiked due to higher import costs.</p>
<p>Hong Kong, however, successfully defended its peg of 7.8 Hong Kong Dollars to one American Dollar - a level which still holds to this day. But this didn't mean that Fragrant Harbour was immune to the financial pain, instead the correction came through brutal internal revaluations, such as the aforementioned property crash. Economic pain wasn't limited to real estate though, the acute internal rebalancing came through higher interest rates, layoffs, downward pressure on wages, rising unemployment and multi-year deflationary pressures.</p>
<p>No economy was immune.</p>
<h3>The Euro Isn't Special</h3>
<p>Hong Kong's peg resembles a currency union, say that of the Eurozone, in many ways. If the currency of a certain economy - say Greece - cannot adjust, then the wages, real estate and other internal prices must do so instead in order to restore competitiveness and correct internal imbalances.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Real Exchange Rate = Nominal Exchange Rate × (Domestic Price Level / Foreign Price Level)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Because of the inability for Eurozone members to see adjustments in their nominal exchange rates, coupled with the generous and calcified social and taxation system of many of the EU's economies, being a member of the Euro results in economic suffering for tens of millions of working-class Europeans. In effect, member countries of the Eurozone ill-suited to the centralised interest rates and exchange rate level are trapped in a painful economic quagmire unable to adjust their currency, but also unable to influence interest rates, and beholden to diktats of the ECB and European Commission. But at the same time, to exit means complete and utter financial ruin, in the words of Greece's ex-finance minister who held office during the Greek Sovereign debt crisis:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"We [Greece] should not have entered the euro – this is crystal clear, but once in, it is disastrous to remove one's-self from the Eurozone voluntarily" - Yanis Varoufakis, Greece's Finance Minister during the European Sovereign Debt Crisis [12]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In early 2026, the incumbent Hungarian government was voted out after 16 years in power. The newly enshrined pro-EU government indicated a desire to follow Bulgaria's footsteps a year earlier, and join the common currency. Bulgaria, only six months after transitioning to the Euro as the currency union's 21st member, was placed under EU deficit procedures [6]. A sign perhaps that the system is working, but also confirmation of the abdication of monetary sovereignty. Austerity has set Europe back decades when compared to the USA [4], but until recently has remained the economic orthodoxy on the continent, no matter the cost.</p>
<p>Hungary, like Poland and Czechia, presently retains its own currency. Hungary has, like much of the former Eastern bloc brought into the EU, seen leaps forward in its standards of living, wages, wealth, etc. since the fall of the Berlin wall. In PPP terms, in 1991, Hungary had a GDP per capita of US$8,352; by 2024 it stood at US$48,552. Poland exceeded this performance and became one of the world's 20 largest economies in 2025 in PPP terms, and now surpasses Spain on a per capita basis (once again, on PPP terms).</p>
<p>Monetary sovereignty may have been a contributor to this success, but not the sole factor. As mentioned, Hungary and Poland started from a relatively low economic base in GDP per capita terms - a bad measure but one which gives some context. Poland, Hungary and other former Eastern bloc EU members have been recipients of significant funding from the EU, supercharging their development.</p>
<p>Hungary also suffers from persistent inflation, and - apparently - the majority of Hungarians might support the introduction of the Euro [9]. Stable savings and lower transaction costs are often cited as reasons to join the common currency.</p>
<p>In reality, a stable currency is within reach for Hungary without joining the Eurozone, and with modern financial solutions and neobanks such as Wise and Revolut, the argument for more efficient transactions is precarious at best - even for businesses subject to cross-border capital flows and revenue streams. Of course, the Eurozone avoids the spreads that come with FX contracts and other currency-risk instruments. But FX costs haven't stopped China and the USA from being Germany's two largest trade partners (three out of Germany's top five trade partners don't use the Euro: China, the USA and Poland) [14].</p>
<p>But whatever the justifications for joining the Eurozone, it is nevertheless imperative that we consider associated costs. To do this, we need only to cast our eyes West.</p>
<h3>The Unnecessary Cost of "Progress"</h3>
<p>Italy enjoyed decades of strong postwar growth, but since 2000, has stagnated, a reality that is seen in Greece and elsewhere at the Eurozone's "periphery".</p>
<p>Whereas under the lira, Italy could devalue from time to time to stay competitive, the currency union renders such an approach impossible. Italy abdicated its monetary sovereignty at the inception of the Euro in 1999. At the same time, Europe's highly regulated economies - relative to other parts of the world - are averse to violent price change mechanisms such as were witnessed in Hong Kong. Unions, minimum wages, and other frictions within the system mean that the adjustment cannot take place in a reasonable time frame. I am not arguing against these measures, but highlighting economic consequences of them. For example, even in Hong Kong, it took 6 years of deflation and internal price changes in the wake of the Asian Financial Crisis, in what is consistently listed as one of the world's freest economies (Hong Kong was ranked the world's no. 1 freest economy in 2023 [3]).</p>
<p>With the lessons of the Asian Financial Crisis in mind, we can better understand the inner workings of the Eurozone economic quagmire (including the sovereign debt crisis). Without a currency to revalue or let float and depreciate, Italy, Greece and the likes saw capital flight within the Eurozone, shifting to "safe" Germany and spiking debt yields across economies perceived as riskier. This suppressed German rates and spiked borrowing costs elsewhere, meaning that the business cycle was accentuated rather than standardised across the currency zone (German banks absorbed capital flight from the periphery via the Euro's TARGET2 settlement system).</p>
<p>In Europe, the consequence of a currency union forced on vastly different economies manifests itself as a cost to prosperity. The IMF has reported extensively, along with other research bodies, that economic hardship correlates with lower fertility levels [10]. Undoubtedly, many countries are experiencing declining birthrates, but to add economic hardship to the mix is hardly a remedy, and has likely exacerbated the trend.</p>
<p>Contrary to common misconceptions, Italians are not lazy, in fact, they work more hours per week than those industrious Germans [8]. Italy still boasts massive engineering prowess and world-class manufacturers. In fact, even in 2026, Italy is Europe's second-largest manufacturing economy (excluding Russia).</p>
<p>However, the structure of this system prevents interest rates from reflecting conditions of the member economies. Germany's interest rates should arguably have been higher, and yet they have been suppressed to support the weaker economies in Europe. The result: a real-estate bubble and significant malinvestment which has been exposed in the past few years and now contributes to escalating deindustrialisation.</p>
<div class="callout callout--note"><div class="callout__header"><span class="callout__type">note</span><span class="callout__title">Germany's own experience with currency transitions is a particularly interesting case, with those in the former East having seen two monetary transitions since 1990, from the Ostdeutschmark to the Deutschmark, and again from the Deutschmark to the Euro. The fact that Germany is a fiscal union, able to transfer spending from West to East (and levy a nationwide Solidarity Tax for East German spending) lessens the cost of rebalancing. But the costs of reunification pervade Germany to this day. It's not all one-way, though. Ironically, because places like Dresden were not immediately rebuilt, the East exhibits more authentic German culture, and often more tastefully restored cities.</span></div></div>
<h3>Envy? China and the USA</h3>
<p>One can surmise that the EU seeks to emulate the success of the other two largest economic trade zones: namely China and the USA. The EU associates scale with power and economic prosperity.</p>
<p>An Optimal Currency Area [7] requires the free flow of labour and capital, fiscal transfer mechanisms (e.g. defence contracts, social security payments in the USA) and similar, synchronised business cycles. Through this lens, Europe's rich tapestry of linguistic, cultural and economic diversity becomes an economic liability rather than an asset (from the perspective of a Eurocrat) [11]. Languages and other national idiosyncrasies are a barrier to the free movement of labour, which is why your next business meeting in Europe will likely be in that simplified form of international English most of the world uses out of sheer practicality. As a result, the EU is structurally predisposed towards internal conformity (and Americanisation), while simultaneously hyping the supposed benefits of "diversity" (perhaps they have a different definition in mind).</p>
<h3>China Opens Up</h3>
<p>In 1978, Deng Xiaoping rose to power in China, and after having been cast out as a "capitalist roader", finally had the chance to implement his reforms of "opening up" [5]. Shenzhen was the first of seven Special Economic Zones (SEZs).</p>
<p><img src="/static/rad-and-deng.png" alt="Deng Xiaoping&#39;s Monument, Lianhuashan Park, 2026" /></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Me in front of monument of Deng Xiaoping, Shenzhen, 2026. Once derided as "artificial" and soulless, this high-tech metropolis makes comparisons with Western cities somewhat embarrassing. After all, every city started somewhere, even Alexandria was planned. While not always feeling totally at home as a Western flaneur navigating its massive scale with broken Mandarin, I nevertheless appreciate its skylines, fleets of electric cars, pristine parks and world-class infrastructure.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Recently I re-visited Shenzhen again after eight years. Today, Shenzhen is one of China's largest cities and an integral part of the Pearl River Delta, an area which boasts around 86 million people and one of the most prosperous economic zones on the planet. It now exceeds most European cities in terms of safety, technology and development. When you visit Shenzhen, you will see murals and statues of Deng, and be hard-pressed to find Mao Zedong.</p>
<p>Deng was able to handle the Optimal Currency Area through free-market principles because mainland China was not only a single monetary union, but also a single political entity. He knew from his time in Moscow in the 1920s, where he had studied at the Sun Yat-sen University, that Lenin's New Economic Policy had injected economic life into the catastrophe of the Bolshevik economy, which lagged well behind pre-revolutionary levels years after the fact [5].</p>
<div class="callout callout--note"><div class="callout__header"><span class="callout__type">note</span><span class="callout__title">Imagine for a moment that China, or the USA, could issue a new currency in their poorer regions, to incentivise capital investment, and more accurately reflect the economic conditions of that state or province. Under such a system, Louisiana or Alabama might see better growth and prosperity, as would the Chinese interior, where the coast is much wealthier on average. Such an experiment could equally be considered in the form of a North Euro and South Euro.</span></div></div>
<p>Even without the flexibility of adjustable internal exchange rates, China and the USA have the political will and fiscal mechanisms to direct investment and policies to adjust for regional disparities. The Eurozone as a "monetary union without a political union" [2] lacks this fundamental tool for internal rebalancing. From the perspective of EU proponents, this can only be overcome through yet more integration and breaking down of economic barriers between its member states - a principle which can be extended beyond the economic realm to language and culture.</p>
<h3>Abolishing the Nations</h3>
<p>Under the current paradigm, nation barriers within Eurozone (and the EU more broadly) represent an obstacle to economic prosperity from the perspective of Brussels. Rather than embrace regional currencies that best reflect the economic circumstances of national economies, they seek to further consolidate control.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>My contention is that free exchange rates are a beautiful mechanism by which countries can rebalance competitiveness without painful internal pricing adjustments. Short of a sovereign currency, smaller economic zones are able to adjust via custom policy and well integrated administrators.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This understanding fails to acknowledge the historic realities that smaller economies are often more prosperous, and their governance more effective, because their administrators are closer to their constituents. In other words, they have "skin in the game" [15]. The Italian Renaissance states, modern Swiss cantons, Singapore, Hong Kong, and Sweden have adapted to crises and emerged as ultra-wealthy societies. Deng was at the helm of a gigantic country of a billion people. His policies worked in spite of China's size because they embraced internal economic freedoms on a smaller province and city scale allowing for policy and price mechanisms prohibited under the preceding Maoist rule.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Deng broke the problem down through smaller, freer Special Economic Zones, in an approach entirely opposite to the EU's process of centralisation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Proponents of the Euro will point to the success of the Baltic club (Latvia, Estonia and Lithuania) which adopted the Euro in 2014, 2011 and 2015 respectively.</p>
<p><img src="/static/gdp-ppp-european-select-economies.PNG" alt="Purchasing power parity; international dollars per capita" /></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Chart of Greece, Italy, Poland, Hungary, and the Baltics. Even in 2000, Italy's per capita GDP was more than double that of the former eastern bloc countries. Today it is only marginally higher than Poland and Lithuania. Greece has fallen below all other countries on the chart save for Latvia. The malaise from the Global Financial Crisis (2008-2009) until 2020 for Greece and Italy manifested itself in persistent unemployment, especially youth unemployment [13].</p>
</blockquote>
<p>But the Baltic states are small with only a few million people each, and have liberalised their economies massively. Being smaller allows them to govern to their specific circumstances, even without sovereign currency flexibility, in a similar fashion to Hong Kong, and not dissimilar to Shenzhen and the other SEZs in China. Smaller might be better as we have seen. Furthermore, their economic prosperity is mostly in line with the success of larger EU economies outside of the Eurozone such as Poland (see chart). Adoption of the Euro is not a prerequisite to success. Their success is owed to sound local governance and EU fiscal support among other factors.</p>
<h3>We Saw This Coming After All</h3>
<p>In his 1997 piece "The Political Economy of the European Economic and Monetary Union", Martin Feldstein accurately presaged the ensuing economic malaise, internal division, unemployment and economic underperformance within Europe. All of this was anticipated and was preventable.</p>
<p>Fundamentally, the Euro is not the cause of Europe's select few success stories. Proponents would likely even acknowledge this, but rather support the Euro's adoption as part of what they deem to be a necessary process of consolidation - which is to say this project is primarily ideologically and politically driven, rather than aiming to prioritise the prosperity of the individual member states and their citizens.</p>
<p>The single currency has at best had marginal impact, and at worst, has imposed massive adjustment costs on member states like Italy through removing their monetary flexibility. Even Germany, often touted as a major beneficiary of the Euro, has experienced malinvestments and a distortion of its currency resulting in lower purchasing power and pervasive bubbles in its economy. ECB interest rates were likely too high for Germany in the early 2000s, and too low in the last decade.</p>
<p>From financial misallocation, chronic underemployment and youth unemployment, plummeting birth rates, and an economic malaise that has lasted a generation, the Euro has hurt rather than benefited many of its members. The EU's attempt to overcome this through further consolidation of centralised power erodes the rich tapestry of culture and thinking originally brought Europe to preeminence. The EU's obsession with centralisation also betrays a naïve and total misunderstanding of the success of China - and also the United States - which I will discuss in another article.</p>
<p>Would Italy have stagnated without the inception of the Euro? Have the Baltics not yet experienced the true costs of the common currency on account of their late adoption into the club? Many questions remain. China leveraged many lessons from the West in its blistering ascendancy in the last few decades. Perhaps it is time for us to learn some lessons from them. For a technocratic system that prides itself on the "science", that the EU has chosen to ignore even elementary economic realities is palpably ironic.</p>
